Natural voice: what it is made of, and what could and could not be checked.

The code here is the repo's own. It loads the packages and the voice below only when a person turns the natural voice
on, and none of them is part of the main bundle.

Packages (npm, exact versions in package.json)
- onnxruntime-web 1.18.0: MIT, Microsoft. Licence field read from the package. Its wasm file is served from this site.
- @diffusionstudio/piper-wasm 1.0.0: MIT, as the package declares. A WebAssembly build of Rhasspy Piper's phonemizer
  (piper_phonemize.js, .wasm, .data, served from this site). The build contains eSpeak NG (the strings "eSpeak-ng" and
  the espeak-ng-data directory are in the files). Upstream publishes eSpeak NG under GPL-3.0-or-later; that was NOT
  confirmed from the sandbox (no access to its repository). Decide whether serving it to browsers is acceptable for
  this site's own licence before the natural voice is released.

Voice (not in the repo; downloaded to the person's browser on opt-in)
- en_US-ljspeech-medium, Rhasspy Piper, from https://huggingface.co/rhasspy/piper-voices (about 63 MB, with its
  settings file). Piper is MIT. The voice is trained on the LJ Speech dataset, which its authors place in the public
  domain; the voice's own model card was NOT readable from the sandbox, so confirm it at
  https://huggingface.co/rhasspy/piper-voices/tree/main/en/en_US/ljspeech/medium before release.
- Set VITE_NATURAL_VOICE_BASE_URL to serve the two files from our own host instead; the opt-in row then names that host.

Nothing the person says or reads is sent anywhere. The only request is the one-time download of the two voice files.
